<article class="env-definition" id="03IX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03IX">Definition <span data-tag="03IX">67.41.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. We say $f$ <em>satisfies the uniqueness part of the valuative criterion</em> if given any commutative solid diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \ar[r] \ar[d] &  X \ar[d] \\  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \ar[r] \ar@{-->}[ru] &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> where $A$ is a valuation ring with field of fractions $K$, there exists at most one dotted arrow (without requiring existence). We say $f$ <em>satisfies the existence part of the valuative criterion</em> if given any solid diagram as above there exists an extension $K'/K$ of fields, a valuation ring $A' \subset K'$ dominating $A$ and a morphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A') \to X$ such that the following diagram commutes </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K') \ar[r] \ar[d] &  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \ar[r] &  X \ar[d] \\  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A') \ar[r] \ar[rru] &  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \ar[r] &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> We say $f$ <em>satisfies the valuative criterion</em> if $f$ satisfies both the existence and uniqueness part. </p>
</article>